int longestPalindrome(string s) {
    int freq[52] = {0}; // frequency of each letter (lowercase and uppercase)
    for(char c : s) {
        if(c >= 'a' && c <= 'z') freq[c - 'a']++; // lowercase letter
        else freq[c - 'A' + 26]++; // uppercase letter
    }
    bool oddFound = false; // whether an odd frequency letter has been found
    int length = 0; // length of the longest palindrome
    for(int i = 0; i < 52; i++) {
        if(freq[i] % 2 == 0) length += freq[i]; // even frequency letter (use all)
        else { // odd frequency letter (use all but one)
            length += freq[i] - 1;
            if(!oddFound) { // use the first odd frequency letter found
                length++; // add one to the length
                oddFound = true; // mark as used
            }
        }
    }
    return length;
}
```

The approach is to count the frequency of each letter (both lowercase and uppercase), and then iterate over them to build the longest palindrome possible. For each letter frequency, if it's even, we can use all occurrences of that letter in the palindrome. If it's odd, we can use all but one occurrence, and we need to keep track of whether we have found an odd frequency letter already, so that we only use one.

